messages:
mike (support): FYI customer seeing duplicate POSTs / double billing when our TS SDK retries a failed request. Node 20, @sam reported SDK v1.6.0, undici 5.10. Customer says it only happens under high upload latency.

jen (support): Ugh. Do we have a repro or logs? Any chance it's the customer re-sending on their side? :thinking_face:

sam (customer): Posting repro snippets below. Server sees two POSTs ~1s apart for a single user action. SDK logs show an abort then a retry. We did not explicitly call retry.

sam (customer): ```ts
// simplified repro client
import fs from 'fs';
import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference-sdk';
const client = new RedwoodClient({ timeoutMs: 700, retry: { maxAttempts: 3, backoffMs: 200 } });
const ac = new AbortController();
// upload large-ish buffer to trigger streaming / slow network
const stream = fs.createReadStream('./big-payload.json');
await client.post('/v1/billing/charge', { body: stream, signal: ac.signal });
```

sam (customer): Server is an express app that intentionally delays ack for 800ms then responds 200. Logs on server: two POSTs received, second arrives after client logs show `request aborted` and `retrying attempt 2`.

maria (eng): Thanks for the repro. My suspicion: the SDK is reusing the same AbortController or same request body stream across retry attempts. With undici pre-5.18 there were known issues where a streamed body can't be re-piped; combined with AbortController reuse a later abort can race and we end up with partial uploads or a second attempt that replays the request in a broken state, sometimes resulting in duplicate server-side effects.

maria (eng): Can you paste the SDK debug logs? Also tell us NODE_OPTIONS / global fetch patching. We had a similar report where globalThis.fetch was polyfilled which interfered with undici streaming semantics.

sam (customer): Logs (redacted):
```
[2026-05-10T13:12:04.123Z] request POST /v1/billing/charge started id=abc123
[2026-05-10T13:12:04.900Z] request aborted id=abc123 reason=timeout
[2026-05-10T13:12:04.902Z] retrying attempt=2 id=abc123
[2026-05-10T13:12:05.050Z] request POST /v1/billing/charge started id=abc123 attempt=2
[2026-05-10T13:12:05.080Z] server accepted charge id=chg_9x
```

sam (customer): We did not see "body clone" logs; looks like attempt 2 actually posted again and the server processed it.

ryan (eng-runtime): This lines up with a classic body/stream reuse + abort race. Two quick asks: 1) can you reproduce with a plain undici fetch (no SDK) so we can isolate, and 2) share exact undici version. If it's <5.18 recommend upgrading.

mike (support): Customer confirmed undici 5.10. They also have some global fetch polyfills in their stack (next.js server middleware).

maria (eng): Workarounds we can suggest immediately:
- Ensure SDK creates a new AbortController per attempt (do not reuse)
- For streaming uploads, convert to a buffer or a repeatable body (e.g., Buffer/Uint8Array) or use a Request factory that creates a fresh Readable for each attempt
- Temporarily pin undici >=5.18 which contains fixes around body cloning/stream semantics

maria (eng): Quick repro using plain undici + a slow server (run locally):
```js
// client-repro.js
import { request } from 'undici';
import fs from 'fs';
const stream = fs.createReadStream('./big-payload.json');
const ac = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 700); // force abort mid-upload
try {
  await request('http://localhost:4000/v1/billing/charge', { method: 'POST', body: stream, signal: ac.signal });
} catch (e) {
  console.error('client error', e.message);
  // naive retry demonstrating issue if body/stream not rewindable
  const stream2 = fs.createReadStream('./big-payload.json');
  await request('http://localhost:4000/v1/billing/charge', { method: 'POST', body: stream2 });
}
```

maria (eng): If the second request succeeds and the server has processed both, you'll see duplicate POST behavior. That proves it's client-side replay of the upload.

jen (support): Suggested message to customer: please try upgrading undici first and, if possible, switch the SDK call to pass a Buffer instead of a stream as a quick mitigation. Also set REDWOOD_SDK_DEBUG=1 and send us the logs if upgrading doesn't help.

sam (customer): Trying upgrade now to undici@>=5.18 and I will report back. If that fails I'll switch to buffer.

maria (eng): On our side, I'll open an internal issue to ensure SDK never reuses the same AbortController across attempts and we properly clone or reject non-rewindable bodies with a clear error message. For streaming bodies we'll return an explicit error recommending a buffer or a user-supplied factory function to produce a fresh stream per retry.

ryan (eng-runtime): Small patch idea for SDK config surface: allow retry.requestFactory = () => ({ body: freshBody(), signal: new AbortController().signal }) so callers can opt-in to a factory. We'll pair that with a lint rule in the SDK that warns when body is a fs.ReadStream.

sam (customer): Upgrade to undici@5.19 resolved it for us in testing — no duplicate POSTs after 30 runs with artificial delay. Thanks!

mike (support): Great. Marking as resolved for customer. Maria/ryan: can you follow up with the SDK PR to add the non-rewindable-body guard? If you'd like I can file an internal support->eng issue with repro and logs.

maria (eng): Will do. I'll create the PR and link the repro. ETA: patch on Monday, will include tests that assert we throw a helpful error when retry attempts would require replaying a non-rewindable stream.

deploy-bot: build 1.6.1-rc.1 triggered for sdk (branch: fix/retry-body-guard)

maria (eng): Thanks all — closing thread after the PR lands and the release is out.
